### 1. Model C1 — Engineering KnowledgeOS / EKS / PKS lineage

The earlier model is fundamentally an **engineering architecture and governance model**.

Its central concern is not "what is knowledge mathematically?" but:

> **How can an engineering system preserve trustworthy knowledge across evidence, authority, identity, transformation, provenance and governance?**

The historical architecture was expressed roughly as:

$$
\text{AI Engineering Platform}
\rightarrow
\text{EKS / PKS}
\rightarrow
\text{KnowledgeOS}
\rightarrow
\text{KnowledgeCore}
\rightarrow
\text{Kernel}
$$

but the corpus explicitly warns that this historical chain mixes ecosystem, domain and kernel views. 

The strongest C1 insight is therefore:

$$
\boxed{\text{Kernel} = \text{invariant-protection mechanism}}
$$

rather than "a container containing knowledge."

The earlier work also identified separation requirements such as:

$$
\text{Confidence}\neq\text{Authority}
$$

$$
\text{Observation}\neq\text{Decision}
$$

$$
\text{Assessment}\neq\text{Truth}
$$

$$
\text{Projection}\neq\text{Source}
$$

and temporal/history separation. 

The six-dimensional kernel candidate that emerged from this work was:

1. Identity preservation
2. Evidence integrity
3. Authority separation
4. Transformation integrity
5. Temporal validity
6. Relationship integrity

with the philosophical lenses used primarily to **discover separation requirements**, not to directly define implementation primitives. 

