# 3. Model M — the mathematical foundation

There is actually a third model emerging, and I think this is the one we need to treat separately.

It is the **mathematical substrate**:

$$
\boxed{(\Omega,\mathcal F,\mathbb P)}
$$

or potentially something more general than a single probability space.

The current proposal says:

$$
\Omega = \text{possible states/observations}
$$

$$
\mathcal F = \text{measurable events}
$$

$$
\mathbb P = \text{probability measure}.
$$

But, as your recent artifact correctly identifies, the critical unresolved question is:

> What exactly is a **knowledge element** in this space?

Possibilities include:

$$
\omega
$$

(outcome),

$$
X:\Omega\rightarrow S
$$

(random variable),

$$
A\in\mathcal F
$$

(event),

$$
f:\Omega\rightarrow S
$$

(measurable function),

or

$$
\mu
$$

(distribution).

These are mathematically different objects.

And importantly, the corpus has already rejected:

$$
\boxed{\text{knowledge projection}=\text{measure}}
$$

as a primitive.

It has also previously declined the idea that there must necessarily be **one universal probability measure**.

So this third model should currently be called something like:

> **M — Mathematical Knowledge Space Model**

rather than prematurely calling it "the KnowledgeOS mathematical model."

